## Why this matters

This refresher exists because a later lesson found a gap: an event handler that always reports the last value of a loop, a timer that reads state that has since changed, or a React-style callback that keeps using an old snapshot. All three are one mechanism: a function captures the variable, not the value it held when the function was created. You do not need the whole chapter on lexical environments again; you need to predict what a captured variable will hold when the function finally runs.

## The simple version

A closure is a function carrying a reference to the room it was born in. It does not take a photograph of the room; it keeps a key. When it runs later, it opens the door and reads whatever the room contains now. `let` in a loop gives each iteration its own room; `var` gives the whole loop one shared room. The analogy breaks for values captured by parameter: a parameter is a new binding on every call, so passing the current value in explicitly is the same as photographing it.

## Core mechanics

Every function is created with a link to the lexical environment where it was defined. Variable lookup walks that chain outward at call time, so a closure sees assignments made after it was created. Each evaluation of a `let` or `const` declaration inside a loop body creates a fresh binding per iteration; the classic `for (let i …)` loop additionally copies `i` into a new binding for each iteration, which is why handlers created in it see distinct values. `var` is function-scoped: one binding, hoisted to the top of the function, shared by every closure created in the loop.

A stale closure is not a bug in the language: the closure faithfully reports the current value of a binding that nobody updated, or an old binding that a new render replaced. The fix is to decide what should be captured: a fresh binding per event (parameters, `let`), a mutable box you update on purpose (an object or a ref), or a subscription that re-reads state when it runs.

## Failure modes

Handlers attached in a `var` loop all report the final index. A timer callback that reads a variable reassigned later prints the new value, which surprises people who expected a snapshot. Debounced or memoized functions that close over configuration keep the configuration from when they were built. Module-level singletons capture the module environment, so tests that mutate module state leak between cases.

## Runnable experiment

Run this with `node` and predict every line before executing it.

```js
const handlers = [];
for (var i = 0; i < 3; i++) handlers.push(() => i);
console.log(handlers.map((h) => h()));      // [3, 3, 3]: one shared binding
const fresh = [];
for (let j = 0; j < 3; j++) fresh.push(() => j);
console.log(fresh.map((h) => h()));         // [0, 1, 2]: a binding per iteration
let count = 0;
const read = () => count;
count = 5;
console.log(read());                        // 5: the closure reads the current value
function snapshot(value) { return () => value; }
const keep = snapshot(count);
count = 9;
console.log(keep());                        // 5: the parameter froze the value
```

## Practical exercise

Take one stale-closure bug from your own code or from the experiment and write three versions: capture a fresh binding per iteration, pass the value as a parameter, and read through a mutable box that you update deliberately. For each version, write a one-line comment saying what the function will see if the outer variable changes later, then prove it with a printed transcript.

exercise.starter_code:
// version 1: fresh binding per iteration
for (let i = 0; i < 3; i++) button(i).onclick = () => report(i);
// version 2: parameter freezes the value
function handlerFor(value) { return () => report(value); }
// version 3: a box updated on purpose
const latest = { value: 0 };
const onTick = () => report(latest.value);
